When I pass the source-gen options through to the converter, it will use them for nested serialization calls. Since `ManagedServiceIdentityType` and `UserAssignedIdentity` are both registered in the source-gen context with their converters, the resolver will find them and use the compiled serialization paths instead of falling back to reflection. So when `ManagedServiceIdentity.Write` gets called directly through the `IJsonModel` path rather than through `JsonSerializer.Serialize`, it receives `null` for the options, which means it falls back to default serialization that isn't AOT-safe. The key difference is that the `IJsonModel` path constructs its own `ModelReaderWriterOptions` instead of relying on the resolver from the context. I need to decide between threading AOT-safe options through all the call paths or replacing the `JsonSerializer` calls with direct writer operations. The `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeV3Converter` appears to handle string serialization, so I could bypass `JsonSerializer` entirely and write the type value directly to the writer instead.

